The chain:
Hellenistic Neoplatonism (Iamblichus, Proclus) is the ancestor of both Ficino and Corbin's systems. Their key doctrine: the soul has a "subtle vehicle" (ochema-pneuma) — not the physical body, not pure intellect, a middle vehicle — and this is where dreams, divination, and daemon-contact happen. Everything downstream is variation on this one move: positing a real ontological third zone between matter and pure mind.
That current splits two ways:

Into Islamicate philosophy — Suhrawardi's Illuminationism, then Ibn Arabi — as alam al-mithal, the imaginal world. Corbin translates this directly: mundus imaginalis. His whole point (stated explicitly against Jung) is that this is not "imaginary" in the fictional sense — it's a real order of being, perceived by imaginatio vera, an organ of cognition, not a psychological projection screen. The beings there (angels, the Daena/soul-guide, daemons) genuinely exist at that ontological rank.
Into Latin Renaissance Platonism — Ficino, De Vita Coelitus Comparanda. Same doctrine, different vocabulary: the cosmos is ensouled, daemones are the graded intermediary class between gods and matter, populating the sublunary space, each planet has its own daemon-train. Phantasia (imagination) is explicitly the faculty that catches celestial influx and gives it shape — the exact organ Corbin later calls imaginal cognition. Ficino's whole talismanic/astrological magic is a technology for drawing that influx down through spiritus (world-spirit) into matter and into you.

Where JSK/goetia fits: it's the same daemonic cosmology's older, cruder, practically-oriented cousin. Goes originally means someone who deals with the restless dead and chthonic powers — not abstract metaphysics, ritual technique. Stratton-Kent's actual scholarly move in Geosophia/Encyclopaedia Goetica is stripping the medieval Solomonic overlay (fallen-angel hierarchies borrowed wholesale from Christian angelology) off the goetic spirits and showing what's underneath: land-spirits, ancestral powers, chthonic daimones tied to specific places (crossroads, rivers, tombs) — continuous with the Greek Magical Papyri and curse tablets, not medieval fiction. Same class of being as Ficino's daemones and Corbin's imaginal denizens; different register — folk/necromantic instead of Platonic-philosophical.
Frater Acher's actual contribution here: he's explicitly fighting the Jungian-reductionist reading (spirits as "just" psyche) and pushing goetic/land-spirit work back toward Corbin-style realism — the being you contact is genuinely other, encountered through the imaginal faculty but not reducible to it. That's why he's the right bridge author for you.
So: where do the entities live? Consistent answer across all four systems — nowhere physical, nowhere "just in your head" either. A real third zone (ochema, alam al-mithal, spiritus mundi, whatever name), accessed only through a trained imaginative faculty, encountered as genuinely autonomous. That's the actual metaphysical claim underneath all of this, stated with more or less directness depending on author.
Steiner's version, concretely:
He'd map it onto his own terms — the etheric and astral bodies are the organs of perception for exactly this zone. His method:

Imaginative cognition first (his term, same idea as Corbin's imaginatio vera) — meditate on a self-chosen symbol/seed-image until it stabilizes into something with its own life, not a picture you're holding by will.
Then inspiration — the image starts communicating, not just being seen.
Then intuition — direct identity/union with what you're perceiving, past the point of separate images.

His one hard, specific warning that the GD tradition doesn't emphasize the same way: the Guardian of the Threshold — a real encountered being (in his account, a form built from your own unresolved lower nature) that meets you at the entry to this zone, and engaging astral/imaginal perception without prior moral schooling (not just technical banishing/purification) risks being deceived by it or by other beings met past it. This is functionally the same phenomenon as the testing/terror encounters goetic evokers report at the edge of an evocation, and the "shadow" material Jungians flag in active imagination — Steiner just makes it an explicit doctrinal checkpoint rather than an incidental risk.
Also relevant and underused by you so far: Paracelsus, direct source for Steiner's elemental-being doctrine (gnomes/undines/sylphs/salamanders as real, place-tied, non-human intelligences) — this is the actual historical bridge between Renaissance daemonology and Steiner's system, and dovetails hard with JSK's land-spirit reading of goetia. Worth reading Liber de Nymphis directly if you want the primary text instead of secondhand summaries.